    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-koch-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "After 0 iterations of the above process there are three line segments. Find a formula for the number of line segments $N_n$ after $n$ iterations.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$N_n=3\\cdot4^n$.",
          "worked": "Each segment is replaced by four segments at every iteration. Starting with three segments gives\n$$\\begin{align*}N_n&=3\\cdot4^n.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-koch-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "After 0 iterations of the above process each line segment has length 1. Find a formula for the length $L_n$ of each line segment after $n$ iterations.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$L_n=\\dfrac{1}{3^n}$.",
          "worked": "Each new segment has one third of the previous segment's length.\n$$\\begin{align*}L_n&=1\\left(\\frac13\\right)^n\\\\L_n&=\\frac{1}{3^n}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-koch-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "After 0 iterations of the above process the perimeter is 3. Find a formula for the perimeter $P_n$ after $n$ iterations.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P_n=3\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^n$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}P_n&=N_nL_n\\\\P_n&=3\\cdot4^n\\cdot\\frac{1}{3^n}\\\\P_n&=3\\left(\\frac43\\right)^n.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-koch-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Show that the perimeter of the Koch snowflake is infinite.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The perimeter is infinite.\n\nSince $\\frac43>1$, $P_n=3(\\frac43)^n$ increases without bound.",
          "worked": "Since $\\frac43>1$,\n$$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}3\\left(\\frac43\\right)^n=\\infty.$$\nThus the perimeter increases without bound, so the Koch snowflake has infinite perimeter.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-koch-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "After 0 iterations of the above process the area is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}$. Find a formula for the area $A_n$ after $n$ iterations.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$A_n=\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{20}\\left(8-3\\left(\\dfrac49\\right)^n\\right)$.\n\nAdd the areas of the $3\\cdot4^{k-1}$ triangles introduced at iteration $k$, each of area $\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4\\cdot9^k}$.",
          "worked": "At iteration $k$, $3\\cdot4^{k-1}$ triangles are added. Each has side length $3^{-k}$ and area $\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4\\cdot9^k}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}A_n&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}+\\sum_{k=1}^{n}3\\cdot4^{k-1}\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4\\cdot9^k}\\\\A_n&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt3}{12}\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\left(\\frac49\\right)^{k-1}\\\\A_n&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt3}{12}\\frac{1-(\\frac49)^n}{1-\\frac49}\\\\A_n&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}+\\frac{3\\sqrt3}{20}\\left(1-\\left(\\frac49\\right)^n\\right)\\\\A_n&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{20}\\left(8-3\\left(\\frac49\\right)^n\\right).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-koch-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Find the area of the Koch snowflake.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{2\\sqrt3}{5}$ square units.\n\nTake the limit of $A_n$, using $(\\frac49)^n\\to0$.",
          "worked": "Since $0<\\frac49<1$, $(\\frac49)^n\\to0$ as $n\\to\\infty$.\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}A_n\\\\A&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{20}(8-0)\\\\A&=\\frac{2\\sqrt3}{5}\\text{ square units}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
